WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Today I'm going to find out with curator

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:04.600
Selina Hurley, how a bed and a bicycle

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.400
led to the creation of the Paralympics.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.600
I recognised Stoke Mandeville from the

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.500
Olympics, so I'm guessing I know what

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.590
this is, but do you want to tell me?

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:18.000
So basically what it is; it's a bed cycle

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.320
and it was developed at Stoke Mandeville Hospital

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.360
by someone called Ludwig Guttmann

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.100
who you can see his name just there.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:26.130
And he developed ideas

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:28.230
that rehabilitation after an injury or

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:29.400
particularly after war injuries should

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:33.360
include physical and emotional [rehabilitation] and work.

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:36.900
So it's basically a bicycle turned on its side?

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.560
Yes. So it's made of lots of different bicycle parts.

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.000
We have the handles here, so you

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.400
would pedal with your hands here.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.400
Or you would pedal with your feet as well.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:48.380
There's actually a little speedometer here

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.660
so you can see how fast you are going.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.500
And you can set how long you would be going for.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:55.500
There's also a gear so you can actually change

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:57.500
the tension like you would on an exercise bike.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:01.640
There's a lot of big, twisty, physical things on this.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.380
So what did this turn into over time?

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:05.820
We know that this one was actually still

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.890
used until 1994 at The Chaseley Trust,

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:09.840
who donated it to the museum.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:12.320
There's obviously lots of different

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:13.900
physio kind of exercises now,

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:16.280
and hydrotherapy as well, but you still have

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.860
forms of exercise bikes that you use today.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.540
But you can just imagine what it

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:22.500
would be like to lie down either on this

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.100
bed, or have this rolled over your bed

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.100
for your daily exercise, whether you

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:29.100
wanted to do it or not.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:32.200
And Stoke Mandeville went on to start the Paralympics is that right?

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.900
It did. So they used to hold

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:35.900
an annual games at Stoke Mandeville

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.240
which was developed by Ludwig Guttmann and his staff

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:40.500
and the eighth version of that games

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.100
turns into the first Paralympic Games.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.640
So, we have a great object that tells some

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.500
really personal stories, but also has

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:49.000
this much bigger link to the Paralympic movement.

